Subject: [#94-1-584] Fractal compression and Internet commerce

Mike L. points out that disk space is cheap and little of it
that is installed on active machines goes unused, so renting it
over the nets is not likely to be cost effective compared to
simply buying local disks.  True... except that the data which
I want to save benefits from being distributed.  Let's say that
my files were something like kiddie porn (they are not, but
pretend)... if they exist only on my local disks, then some
nice people from the government will eventually show up and
take my toys away.  It would be awfully difficult for them
to go to maybe 100 sites scattered around the world and
shut them down.
