Subject: [#94-1-586] PHIL: faith, reason, mysticism

Ray Cromwell writes --

>   Could it be that MYSTICISM hasn't produced anything useful lately?
> When was the last time it cured cancer, launched a communications satelilite,
> revived someone from biostasis, or multiplied intelligence by a thousand
> fold?

cured cancer: I don't know; you will claim that it never has done so,
many others will claim the same, many others will claim the opposite;
I find no convincing evidence either way

launched a satellite: never

revived someone from biostasis: quite a common practice among the fakirs
of India, whether you believe it or not

multiplied intelligence by a thousand fold: I assume you mean in a
single individual; never; but show me when reason has done this

anything useful: relieved my stress-induced health problems last week

   > Maybe you need to admit to yourself that we have different standards
> of usefulness.

You bet.  I find mental calm, clear-headed thinking and the ability to
look at a situation objectively very useful.  Meditation helps me with
all of these things, and meditation is considered by many to be a
'mystical' practice.

  > An objectivist railing against reason? Is the world coming to an end?

  There is a big difference (in my mind at least) between an objectivist
  (note the lower case o) and an Objectivist (note the upper case O and
  read as 'Ayn Rand cultist who believes everything she ever wrote as
  dogma').

I do not believe that all thought can be broken into only 2 camps,
rational or irrational.  I believe a third category of 'nonrational'
exists, is quite useful and in fact is the most common mode of thinking.
I do not 'rail against reason'; I rail against the unreasonable notion
that reason is the only viable mode of acquiring knowledge or changing
one's mental state (other than perhaps drugs).
